steps.body:
Every function call gets a **stack frame**: a block of memory that holds its parameters and local variables. The frame appears when the call starts and is popped when it returns. Creating or popping a frame just moves one pointer, which is why stack memory is so fast. Each local is destroyed at the closing brace of the block that declares it.

An inner block may declare a name that already exists outside. The inner variable **shadows** the outer one until its brace closes. It is legal but easy to misread, so use it sparingly.

```cpp
int level = 1;
{
    int level = 2; // a second, separate variable
}
std::cout << level << '\n';  // 1 again
```

Each call gets a fresh frame, so a plain local restarts every time. A `static` local is created once, lives outside the stack for the whole program, and keeps its value between calls.

```cpp
int countCalls() {
    static int calls = 0; // initialized once, not on every call
    ++calls;
    return calls;
}
countCalls();
std::cout << countCalls() << '\n';  // 2, calls kept its value
```

Never return a pointer or reference to a local: its frame is gone after `return`, so the caller holds a dangling address (undefined behavior). The stack is also small, 8 MB here. A local array bigger than that, such as `int big[3'000'000];` (12 MB), crashes the program, often before anything prints. Big buffers belong in a `std::vector`, whose elements live on the heap.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int countCalls() {
    static int calls = 0;
    ++calls;
    return calls;
}

int freshCount() {
    int calls = 0;
    ++calls;
    return calls;
}

int main() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        std::cout << countCalls() << (i < 2 ? ' ' : '\n');
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        std::cout << freshCount() << (i < 2 ? ' ' : '\n');
    }
    int level = 1;
    {
        int level = 2;
        std::cout << "inside: " << level << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "after: " << level << '\n';
    return 0;
}

