steps.body:
`new int[n]` allocates `n` ints in one contiguous heap block and returns a pointer to the first one. Unlike `int a[5]`, the count can be a value computed while the program runs. Index the result like any array. Watch the brackets: `new int(5)` is one int holding 5, while `new int[5]` is five ints.

```cpp
const int n = 6;
int* squares = new int[n];  // 6 ints holding garbage
int* zeros = new int[4]();  // () zero-initializes
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    std::cout << zeros[i] << ' ';  // 0 0 0 0
}
delete[] squares;
delete[] zeros;
```

Three rules to remember:

- Plain `new int[n]` leaves the elements uninitialized. Fill them before reading, or add `()` to start them at zero.
- Free an array with `delete[]`, never plain `delete`. Mixing them up is undefined behavior.
- The pointer does not remember how many elements it points to. Keep `n` in a variable and pass it along with the pointer, as in `printArray(squares, n)`.

That is exactly the bookkeeping `std::vector` does for you: it allocates its own heap buffer, remembers the size, and frees the buffer automatically when it goes out of scope. Use `std::vector` in real code and raw `new[]` only when you need to see the mechanics.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

void printArray(const int* arr, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << (i < n - 1 ? ' ' : '\n');
    }
}

int main() {
    const int n = 6;
    int* squares = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        squares[i] = i * i;
    }
    printArray(squares, n);
    delete[] squares;
    squares = nullptr;

    int* zeros = new int[4]();
    printArray(zeros, 4);
    delete[] zeros;
    zeros = nullptr;
    return 0;
}

